"""Build the navigation and study pages for the public synthetic edition."""
from course_common import DIST,shell,esc,table
LESSONS=[
('Can we trust the data?','Define the unit, inspect the key, and leave an auditable trail.',18),
('Distributions and association','Read variation, compare denominators, and separate association from action.',24),
('Generalize and test','Explain uncertainty, design a test, and keep precision separate from relevance.',24),
('Model a relationship','Fit an interpretable relationship and challenge its scale and shape.',24),
('Qualify and extend a regression','Distinguish conditional comparisons, uncertainty, and predictive claims.',24),
('Build and validate predictions','Choose a loss, prevent leakage, and evaluate a frozen procedure.',24),
('Causal questions and experiments','Define counterfactuals and connect a randomized design to a decision.',24),
('Causal graphs and adjustment','Use a causal story to decide what conditioning helps and what it harms.',28),
('Matching and regression adjustment','Make the target population, support, and identifying assumptions visible.',28),
('Difference-in-differences','Build a counterfactual trend and audit the design behind the contrast.',28)]
def page(name,title,body): (DIST/name).write_text(shell(title,body),encoding='utf-8',newline='\n')
def document(title,lead,sections):
 return '<main id="main"><div class="hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Data to decisions · Public synthetic edition</p><h1>'+esc(title)+'</h1><p class="lead">'+lead+'</p></div><div class="content wrap">'+''.join('<section id="'+sid+'"><h2>'+heading+'</h2>'+text+'</section>' for sid,heading,text in sections)+'</div></main>'
art='<div class="display-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 340" role="img" aria-label="A conceptual path from observations through a comparison to a decision"><title>From observations to a defensible decision</title><path d="M65 66C270 66 60 178 230 178S85 275 160 284" stroke="#276451" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 5" fill="none"/><circle cx="65" cy="66" r="40" fill="#276451"/><text x="65" y="73" text-anchor="middle" fill="white" font-size="22">01</text><circle cx="230" cy="178" r="40" fill="#9b421e"/><text x="230" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="white" font-size="22">02</text><circle cx="160" cy="284" r="40" fill="#193d35"/><text x="160" y="291" text-anchor="middle" fill="white" font-size="22">03</text><text x="118" y="58" fill="#193d35" font-size="16">Observe carefully</text><text x="15" y="177" fill="#193d35" font-size="16">Compare fairly</text><text x="10" y="283" fill="#193d35" font-size="16">Decide</text></svg></div>'
cards=''.join(f'<a class="card" href="lesson-{i}.html"><p class="eyebrow">Lesson {i:02d}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{desc}</p><span class="meta">Short case · Guided lesson · Technical appendix →</span></a>' for i,(title,desc,stops) in enumerate(LESSONS,1))
page('index.html','Better questions. Defensible decisions.',f'<main id="main"><div class="hero wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">An applied business statistics course</p><h1>Better decisions begin with better questions.</h1><p class="lead">Learn to turn a business question into evidence you can explain, reproduce, and challenge.</p><div class="downloads"><a class="button" href="lesson-1.html">Start with the data</a><a href="study-guide.html">Find your learning route →</a></div></div>{art}</div><div class="wrap"><div class="notice"><strong>A new, fully synthetic public edition.</strong> Every case dataset is created from a documented model and fixed seed. Charts, worked results, and downloadable labs use those generated observations, not historical source data.</div><section id="learning-map"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Ten business lessons · Three depths of study</p><h2>Your learning path.</h2><p>Start with a short business decision. Continue through a guided worked case when you want more practice. Use the technical appendix for generation rules, derivations and Python labs.</p></div><div class="cards">{cards}</div></section><section class="section-head"><h2>Put the methods together.</h2><p>A strong analysis names its target, comparison, uncertainty, and limitations before recommending an action.</p><div class="downloads"><a class="button" href="synthesis.html">Work through the synthesis</a><a href="resources.html">Data, assumptions, and lab files →</a></div></section><p style="margin:35px 0">No account is required. Your reading progress and answers are not stored or transmitted by this site.</p></div></main>')
page('study-guide.html','One good session at a time.',document('One good session at a time.','The goal is not to memorize a test menu. It is to explain why a particular comparison answers a particular question.',[
('session','Build a useful study session.','<ol><li><strong>State the question.</strong> What decision would change if the answer were different?</li><li><strong>Read one short business case.</strong> State the comparison in your own words.</li><li><strong>Try the decision question.</strong> Answer before opening the reasoning and generator truth.</li><li><strong>Follow the guided lesson.</strong> Work through selected charts, calculations and decision checks.</li><li><strong>Open the technical appendix or lab.</strong> First change only the random seed; then restore it and change one model assumption. Explain what moves in each run.</li></ol><p>A new seed draws another sample from the same stipulated model and reveals sampling variation. Changing a model assumption tests sensitivity to a different stipulated world. Stability across seeds cannot establish that the model describes a real population.</p>'),
('routes','Choose a route, not a shortcut.',table(['Purpose','Suggested route'],[['Build foundations','Lessons 1–3, then 4–6'],['Evaluate a predictive system','Lessons 1, 4–6; revisit Lesson 3 for uncertainty'],['Evaluate an intervention','Lessons 1, 3, 7–10'],['Review an analysis','Start with the synthesis; revisit assumptions in each linked lesson']],'Learning routes')+'<p>These routes organize attention. They do not remove prerequisites: a regression coefficient still needs a measurement definition, and a causal claim still needs a credible comparison.</p>'),
('notebook','Read and rerun the same calculation.','<p>Each lesson includes a Python script, notebook, and calculated-results JSON. The notebook contains actual output from its accompanying script. Each short case links to a guided lesson with selected worked steps and a technical appendix with the data-generating process, the known synthetic truth, and the complete methods.</p><p><a href="python-bridge.html">Use the Python bridge</a> if arrays, functions, or environments are new to you. You can understand the main explanations without running code.</p>'),
('limits','Separate four kinds of confidence.','<ul><li><strong>Arithmetic:</strong> does the calculation match the stated formula?</li><li><strong>Statistical:</strong> does the interval or test fit the sampling assumptions?</li><li><strong>Design:</strong> does the comparison identify the desired effect?</li><li><strong>External:</strong> could the result carry to the people and conditions in your decision?</li></ul><p>A correct synthetic calculation establishes a method illustration, not an empirical recommendation about a real business.</p>')]))
resources='<p>These files require no source-data downloads or login. Each script generates its own observations, computes its estimates and prints JSON. Random seeds and model assumptions are in the source.</p>'+table(['Lesson','Notebook','Python script','Results'],[[str(i),f'Lesson {i} notebook',f'Lesson {i} script',f'Lesson {i} JSON'] for i in range(1,11)],'Lab inventory')
resources='<p>Each lab creates its own observations from a fixed seed. No historical datasets, trained fits, or private source archives are distributed.</p><ul>'+''.join(f'<li><strong>Lesson {i}: {esc(t)}</strong><br><a href="assets/lesson{i}-lab.ipynb" download>Notebook</a> · <a href="assets/lesson{i}-lab.py" download>Python script</a> · <a href="assets/lesson{i}-results.json">Calculated results</a></li>' for i,(t,_,__) in enumerate(LESSONS,1))+'</ul>'
page('resources.html','Data, assumptions, and reproduction.',document('Data, assumptions, and reproduction.','Know where every result comes from before using it.',[
('labs','Run the complete lesson labs.',resources),
('provenance','Original synthetic data, not disguised observations.','<p>The public edition uses new fictional case data generated solely for teaching. The generators stipulate distributions, coefficients, assignment rules and noise. They do not resample, fit to, or approximate the course’s earlier historical source records.</p><p>Names identify fictional scenarios, not actual people, companies, cities or markets. All displayed case estimates and chart values are calculated from the new generators. Pure mathematical examples and formulas are labeled by their role.</p><p>External data licensing is therefore not a prerequisite to running these public labs. Synthetic does not mean realistic, and a known simulation mechanism does not establish how any real system works.</p>'),
('reproduce','Reproduce from a fresh environment.','<p>Use Python 3.11 or later with NumPy, pandas, SciPy and scikit-learn. The repository records the tested dependency versions. No network is used by the labs after those standard packages are installed.</p><pre><code>python -m venv .venv\n# Activate the environment for your operating system.\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\npython scripts/build_all.py\npython scripts/check_public_release.py</code></pre><p>Run an individual downloaded script with Python, or open its notebook in a Jupyter-compatible environment. Run the code from a fresh kernel. Floating-point results may vary slightly across library versions, so compare to the precision shown rather than expecting identical last digits across every platform.</p>'),
('interpretation','Use the examples for methods, not empirical claims.','<p>Choose the real decision’s population, measurement process and assignment mechanism independently. A simulation can reveal an estimator’s behavior under its stated assumptions. It cannot prove those assumptions hold in a business, medical or policy setting.</p><p>The private historical course edition has not been overwritten. This public edition is a distinct teaching release with different data and recalculated results.</p>'),
('reading','Further reading.','<ul><li><a href="https://gabors-data-analysis.com/">Békés and Kézdi: Data Analysis for Business, Economics, and Policy</a> for applied analytical reasoning.</li><li><a href="https://www.openintro.org/book/os/">OpenIntro Statistics</a> for statistical foundations.</li><li><a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html">scikit-learn: common pitfalls</a> for validation and leakage.</li><li><a href="https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/miguel-hernan/causal-inference-book/">Hernán and Robins: Causal Inference</a> for causal identification.</li></ul><p>These links are optional readings, not data sources for the generated cases. Their materials keep their own terms.</p>'),
('access','Learning access.','<p>All text, answers, navigation, tables and downloadable files work without an account. Lessons are readable with JavaScript disabled. Charts have text descriptions and corresponding data tables. Roadmap links, answer disclosures and downloads are keyboard accessible. On small screens, open the lesson roadmap to choose a stop.</p><p>The website itself has no analytics, login, cookies, or answer-submission service. GitHub’s hosting infrastructure has its own privacy terms.</p>')]))
page('python-bridge.html','A small Python bridge.',document('A small Python bridge.','Read the statistical idea first. Then use code to make the calculation inspectable.',[
('objects','A value, an array, and a named result.','<p>A variable is a label for an object. A list stores a sequence. NumPy arrays add efficient elementwise arithmetic. The following five values are invented for this demonstration.</p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nx = np.array([8., 11., 12., 14., 20.])\nmean = x.mean()\nse = x.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(len(x))\nprint(mean, se)</code></pre><p>The sample mean is 13. The sample variance is 20, so the standard error of the mean under independent sampling is 2. An array operation does not check whether independence is plausible.</p>'),
('seed','A seed makes a simulation repeatable.','<pre><code>rng = np.random.default_rng(204)\ndraw = rng.normal(loc=10, scale=2, size=100)</code></pre><p>The code stipulates a normal population with mean 10 and standard deviation 2. The sample mean need not be 10. Reusing the seed and supported library versions reproduces the same draw; choosing a new seed demonstrates sampling variation.</p>'),
('functions','Functions keep assumptions in one place.','<pre><code>def sample_mean(values):\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=float)\n    if values.size == 0:\n        raise ValueError("A mean needs observations")\n    return values.mean()</code></pre><p>A function has inputs and an output. An assertion checks a property you expect to hold. Comments should explain why a step is appropriate, not just translate the syntax.</p>'),
('results','Follow the data to the claim.','<ol><li>Read the generating assumptions and seed.</li><li>Find the definition of the outcome and unit.</li><li>Check which rows enter each fit or comparison.</li><li>Locate the calculation that prints the displayed result.</li><li>Change one assumption, rerun all code, and explain the difference.</li></ol><p><a href="resources.html#labs">Choose a lesson notebook →</a></p>')]))
page('synthesis.html','One decision. Different evidence.',document('One decision. Different evidence.','Fictional business scenario: a distributor is considering replenishment alerts for its branches. No numerical claim here describes an actual organization.',[
('decision','Specify the action and the target.','<p>The action is an alert policy with a stated threshold, delivery channel, and response deadline. The outcome could be stockout days per branch-month. The causal target might be the average effect of offering the policy to eligible branches over the next quarter, not the effect among only the staff who respond.</p><p>Before analyzing, record the cost of a missed stockout, unnecessary restocking, staff burden and implementation. A statistically detectable effect need not justify the cost.</p>'),
('measure','First make the rows defensible.','<p>Audit branch identifiers, product coverage, calendar definitions and missing stock records. A branch with no record is not necessarily a branch with no stockouts. A join between orders and inventory can duplicate a branch-month.</p><details><summary>What evidence would you ask for?</summary><div><p>A unit and key specification, unmatched-row report, source coverage by month, a missingness policy and an explanation of denominator changes. See <a href="lesson-1.html">Lesson 1</a>.</p></div></details>'),
('predict','Predicting risk does not evaluate the intervention.','<p>A model can rank branches likely to have stockouts using information available before the alert decision. Validate by time if future deployment is the target. Select models without using the final evaluation period, and evaluate the loss implied by the business costs.</p><details><summary>Can a highly accurate risk score prove alerts work?</summary><div><p>No. It estimates a prediction function under observed conditions. An intervention changes behavior and may change the data distribution. See <a href="lesson-6.html">Lesson 6</a>.</p></div></details>'),
('experiment','Randomize when the design is feasible.','<p>Randomly assign eligible branches to receive the alert policy or usual operations. Define the allocation unit, spillover risks, primary outcome, analysis window and stopping rule before outcomes are observed. If staff work across branches, cluster the assignment appropriately.</p><details><summary>Which comparison matches offering the policy?</summary><div><p>Compare outcomes by randomized offer, including branches that ignore the alerts. This is an intention-to-treat effect of the offer. Effects among users need additional assumptions. See <a href="lesson-7.html">Lesson 7</a>.</p></div></details>'),
('observe','If selection is observational, make the assumptions visible.','<p>Managers may choose alerts for branches with worsening stockouts. Compare pre-treatment common causes, inspect overlap, and define whether the target is treated branches or all eligible branches. Do not control for staff response if it lies after the alert.</p><details><summary>What does better covariate balance establish?</summary><div><p>It shows measured comparisons improved under a specified weighting or matching procedure. It does not demonstrate the absence of unmeasured common causes. See <a href="lesson-8.html">Lesson 8</a> and <a href="lesson-9.html">Lesson 9</a>.</p></div></details>'),
('trends','With a rollout, ask about counterfactual trends.','<p>A before–after drop can reflect seasonality, supply recovery, or a changed product mix. A comparison group can help only if it supplies a plausible untreated trend for the treated branches. Check timing, anticipation, spillovers and concurrent initiatives.</p><details><summary>Do similar pre-trends prove parallel untreated trends?</summary><div><p>No. They can challenge or support a design argument, but post-period untreated outcomes for treated branches remain unobserved. See <a href="lesson-10.html">Lesson 10</a>.</p></div></details>'),
('brief','Write a recommendation someone can challenge.','<ol><li><strong>Decision:</strong> the policy and population.</li><li><strong>Estimand:</strong> exactly what difference you seek.</li><li><strong>Design:</strong> why the comparison could identify it.</li><li><strong>Estimate:</strong> the effect or prediction in useful units.</li><li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> sampling, design and transfer limits.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> the cost-sensitive choice and what would change it.</li></ol><p>For this fictional scenario, no data have been supplied to estimate an effect. The defensible output is a design and analysis plan, not a made-up recommendation to deploy.</p>')]))
